{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the journeys.</b> Why were these journeys written? To make known the lovingkindness of the Omnipresent — that even though He decreed upon them to wander and to move them about in the wilderness, do not say that they were continually journeying and wandering from station to station all forty years with no rest, for there are only forty-two journeys here. Subtract from them fourteen, all of which were in the first year, before the decree — from when they journeyed from Rameses until they came to Ritmah, for from there the spies were dispatched, as it says, ״וְאַחַר נָסְעוּ הָעָם מֵחֲצֵרוֹת״ (Numbers 12), ״שְׁלַח לְךָ אֲנָשִׁים״ (Numbers 13), and here (verse 18) it says, ״וַיִּסְעוּ מֵחֲצֵרֹת וַיַּחֲנוּ בְּרִתְמָה״ — you learn that it is in the wilderness of Paran. And further subtract from there eight journeys that were after the death of Aharon — from Hor HaHar to the plains of Moav — in the fortieth year. It turns out that all thirty-eight years they journeyed only twenty journeys. This is from the foundation of Rabbi Moshe. And Rabbi Tanchuma expounded it with a different exposition: A parable — to a king whose son was ill, and he took him to a far place to heal him. When they were returning, his father began to count all the journeys: he said to him, Here we slept, here we were cold, here your head ached, and so forth."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:39.668337694Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}